{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Zebulun shall dwell at the shore of the sea, etc.</b> Since Zebulun was engaged in commerce and would place sustenance into the mouth of Issachar, who was engaged in Torah, Moses too said: ״שְׂמַח זְבוּלוּן בְּצֵאתֶךָ״ (Deuteronomy 33:18) — for money has no perfection except through expenditure, and the expenditure is good in it more than the gathering; but ״יִשָּׂשכָר בְּאֹהָלֶיךָ״ (Deuteronomy 33:18), for the acquisition of money does not adhere to him as does the acquisition of Torah, which is always in his tent and does not depend on expenditure, for even if the acquisition remains in his tent it has perfection. And so this verse comes to reveal the deficiency bound up with those engaged in the acquisition of money, for apart from the fact that all their days they have no rest, there is in them yet one further lack — and that is the scattering of the soul. For he and his household and his merchandise are scattered and separated one from another: the dwelling of his house is in one place, and he himself is on the road or in a ship in the heart of the sea, and his merchandise in a third place — and behold, his soul is scattered, for his thoughts are upon his house in the east, and his thoughts are upon his merchandise in the west, and his thoughts are upon himself between these two borders. This is what it says: ״זְבוּלוּן לְחוֹף יַמִּים יִשְׁכּוֹן״ — for there is his house, but he does not dwell in his house; rather, ״הוּא לְחוֹף אֳנִיּוֹת״ (Genesis 49:13), for all his days he is in a ship in the heart of the sea; ״וְיַרְכָתוֹ״ — that is, his merchandise and his money, which is called ״הַיְקוּם אֲשֶׁר בְּרַגְלָיו״ — that which he sets up on his legs; likewise this is what stands a man up and raises him — ״עַל צִידוֹן״ (Genesis 49:13), for it is a place of commerce, as it says (Isaiah 23:2): ״סֹחֵר צִידוֹן עֹבֵר יָם מִלְאוּךְ״. And concerning this one sage said: may HaShem deliver him from the scattering of the soul, for his thoughts are scattered in many places. And he mentioned the opposite regarding Issachar, as will be explained in what follows."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The phrase הַיְקוּם אֲשֶׁר בְּרַגְלָיו appears to be a paraphrased allusion to Deuteronomy 33:28 (וּבְטַח רֹבֵץ יַעֲקֹב אֶל דֶּשֶׁן אֶרֶץ וְשָׁמַיִם עֲלֵיהֶם טַל וְכו׳), but more likely echoes the idiom of 'what stands on its legs' for ready money; retained hyper-literally as the text is compressed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:34.378616820Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}